        "math_academy_id": "q-4814",
        "knowledge_point_id": "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92",
        "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative",
        "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-6$ and $f(1)=-2.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(2).$",
        "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=-2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-6,$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=12(1)^{2}-6=6.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-2+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ≈L(2) \\\\  & =-2+6(2-1) \\\\  & =-2+6(1) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
