        "knowledge_point": "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate",
        "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}.",
        "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$",
