      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-73342",
        "knowledge_point_id": "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072",
        "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals",
        "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        "worked_solution": "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+2={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}+4(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =5(1-(-2))+10 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
